notes.body:
> I meant if the standard describes the octet string coding in any way.

If this is based on ISO 9506-2, then FloatingPoint seems to be defined as:

- an octet indicating how many bits wide the exponent is;
- an octet whose uppermost bit is the sign bit of the number and the remaining bits are part of a bit string;
- subsequent octets that continue the bit string.

If the exponent width is *n*, the first *n* bits of the bit string are the exponent and the remaining bits are the mantissa.

The interpretation of the exponent and mantissa are such that:

- if the exponent width is 8 and the octet string has 5 octets including the exponent width, the number is an IEEE 754 single-precision floating-point number;
- if the exponent width is 11 and the octet string has 9 octets including the exponent width, the number is an IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point number;

but there's no guarantee that those will be true.

See section 14.4.2.2 "The FloatingPoint Type" in ISO 9506-2, Second Edition, 2003-07-01.

So I'd say that:

1. there should be no preference to control whether to show the value as an octet string or as a floating-point number - we should always show it as a floating-point number;
1. we should, at minimum, handle the double-precision case in addition to the single-precision case;
1. if the number of exponent bits is neither 8 with a total length of 5 octets or 11 with a total length of 9 octets, we should just treat it as unrepresentable and show it as an octet string;
1. at some point, if that's the case we should pull the bits out and put the result into a double-precision value as long as the exponent fits in 11 bits and the mantissa fits in 52 bits (not counting the sign bit), and otherwise treat it as unrepresentable and show it as an octet string.

